---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { guides } from '../../data/guides';

const guide = guides.find((g) => g.slug === 'how-to-clean-airpods-speaker-mesh')!;
const url = `https://fixthespeaker.com/guides/${guide.slug}`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: guide.title,
  description: guide.description,
  datePublished: guide.published,
  dateModified: guide.updated,
  mainEntityOfPage: url,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Dev', url: 'https://fixthespeaker.com/about' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://fixthespeaker.com/#organization' },
};
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${guide.title} | Fix The Speaker`}
  description={guide.description}
  heading={guide.title}
  eyebrow="Guide"
  updated={guide.updated}
  localized={false}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>
    <strong>Short answer:</strong> for everyday dust, a dry, soft-bristled brush is enough. For built-up earwax,
    Apple now publishes a mesh-cleaning method for recent AirPods models that uses micellar water, distilled water
    and a soft children's toothbrush. Whatever you do, never use sharp objects, never run AirPods under the tap,
    and let them dry for at least two hours before you put them back in the case.
  </p>

  <h2>Why one AirPod gets quieter</h2>
  <p>
    The speaker in each AirPod sits behind a fine mesh. Earwax, skin oil and sweat slowly cover that mesh, and
    because you usually favour one ear, one side often clogs first. The result is an AirPod that sounds quieter,
    thinner or muffled compared with the other one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before cleaning, rule out the easy causes: check the balance slider in your phone's accessibility audio
    settings, and make sure both AirPods are charged and connected.
  </p>

  <h2>Option 1: a dry brush (for light dust and lint)</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Take the AirPods out of the case. On models with ear tips, pull the tips off.</li>
    <li>
      Hold the AirPod with the mesh facing down and brush lightly with a small, clean, dry, soft-bristled brush.
      Facing down lets debris fall away instead of deeper in.
    </li>
    <li>Wipe the outside with a soft, dry, lint-free cloth.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>This is safe to do often and is all most people need.</p>

  <h2>Option 2: Apple's mesh-cleaning method (for earwax)</h2>
  <p>
    Apple's support pages for AirPods and AirPods Pro describe a deeper clean for the meshes on recent models. The
    exact meshes to clean, and which models it applies to, are shown in pictures on Apple's pages, so open the page
    for your model (linked below) and follow along. In outline:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Get the supplies.</strong> Micellar water that lists PEG-6 Caprylic/Capric Glycerides as an ingredient,
      distilled water, a soft-bristled children's toothbrush, two small cups and a paper towel. Apple also mentions a
      Belkin cleaning kit made for AirPods as an alternative.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Brush with micellar water.</strong> Dip the brush, then brush only the meshes Apple marks, in small
      circles for about 15 seconds. Blot the AirPod on the paper towel, mesh down. Apple says to repeat this three
      times.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Rinse with distilled water</strong> the same way, using the second cup.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Let them dry.</strong> Apple says to allow at least two hours before charging them or wearing them.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Do not clean any other part of the AirPod this way. Apple is specific that only the marked meshes should be
    cleaned.
  </p>

  <h2>Ear tips and the charging case</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Ear tips (AirPods Pro):</strong> pull them off and rinse them with water only, no soap or household
      cleaners. Make sure they are completely dry before you put them back on.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Charging case:</strong> wipe it with a soft, dry, lint-free cloth (Apple says you can slightly dampen
      the cloth with isopropyl alcohol if needed). Clear the charging port with a dry, soft brush, and keep all
      liquid out of it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What not to do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No toothpicks, pins or needles.</strong> Apple warns against sharp objects and abrasive materials. A
      punctured mesh lets wax reach the speaker itself.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Do not run them under the tap</strong> or soak them.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not put them back in the case wet.</strong> Moisture in the case can reach the charging contacts.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Do not play loud test tones while wearing them.</strong> If you use any sound to check them, take
      them out of your ears first.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Can a sound clean AirPods?</h2>
  <p>
    Not much. A tone can vibrate the mesh and may loosen a little light dust, which is why our
    <a href="/clean-airpods-speaker">AirPods page</a> offers one, always played with the AirPods out of your ears.
    But earwax is sticky, and sound will not remove it. Use the brush or Apple's method above.
  </p>

  <h2>When to get them checked</h2>
  <p>
    If one AirPod is still quiet after a careful clean and two hours of drying, or it crackles, the problem may be
    inside. Contact Apple Support; replacing a single AirPod is often possible.
  </p>

